// Change: new rule
// Title: Service Environment Variables Set Via Registry
// Sigma ID: 0c8f2336-9fe3-467d-9a1b-efbba1862d74
// Status: experimental | Level: medium
// Author: Cory Burrell
// Date: 2026-11-14 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: execution, stealth, T1574.012, T1574.014
// Description: Detects a write to the "Environment" value of a Windows service. This REG_MULTI_SZ value holds
//   environment variables that the service process receives at start. Attackers can use it to set
//   .NET variables such as "COR_ENABLE_PROFILING" and "COR_PROFILER", or "APPDOMAIN_MANAGER_ASM" and
//   "APPDOMAIN_MANAGER_TYPE", for one service only, so that a trusted service process loads their
//   code. Sysmon logs the data of this value as "Binary Data", so rules that look for the variable
//   names in the registry cannot see it.
// False positives:
//   - Application performance monitoring and profiling agents (for example Datadog, New Relic, OpenTelemetry or Visual Studio IntelliTrace) that set environment variables for a service such as IIS (W3SVC, WAS)
// References:
//   - https://www.zscaler.com/blogs/cybersecurity-best-practices/cor_profiler-for-persistence
//   - https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-dotnet-instrumentation/blob/cb157b1b05f327011fb0eb298aeaee31815072a7/docs/windows-service-instrumentation.md
//   - https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/archive/blogs/msaffer/using-intellitrace-with-services
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/744ff47b0a7bf6528913f5c6a0d08c2b02f40884/rules/windows/registry/registry_set/registry_set_service_environment_value_set.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.
// Note: 1 wildcard comparison(s) rewritten to 'matches regex' (KQL treats * in contains/endswith/== as a literal character)

DeviceRegistryEvents
| where RegistryKey has_all ("HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM*") and RegistryKey matches regex @"(?i)\\Services.*$" and RegistryKey endswith "\\Environment"
